Phrase models for this fork's own voice worker (src/voice-wakeword.ts)

Not part of the add-voice-mode skill: the skill copies an explicit file list and
never this directory, and its default wake word stays hey_livekit. This fork's
deploy opts in through .env, with paths relative to the NanoClaw directory:

  VOICE_WAKE_MODEL=assets/voice-commands/hey_dan.onnx
  VOICE_DISCARD_MODEL=assets/voice-commands/scratch_that.onnx

hey_dan.onnx listens for "hey dan" (and "hey den", as a Ukrainian speaker says
it). It is a livekit-wakeword classifier (conv-attention, medium) trained with
livekit-wakeword (https://github.com/livekit/livekit-wakeword, Apache-2.0,
commit fb92cb3 with local changes for weighted hand-picked near misses and
batched feature extraction) on synthetic speech only:
- 50,000 positive and 50,000 adversarial clips from the Piper VITS
  en-us-libritts-high voice that livekit-wakeword downloads, with 40 hand-picked
  near misses ("hey ben", "hey stan", "hey danny", "send it", ...);
- background noise, room responses and the ACAV100M negative features that
  livekit-wakeword's setup downloads;
- 100,000 training steps, target 0.1 false positives per hour.
It runs on the mel and embedding models in assets/voice-wakeword/.

Its own evaluation (35 h of held-out negatives): recall 0.885 at 0.5 with 0.49
false positives per hour; recall 0.81 at 0.76 with 0.086. Its known threshold
in the worker is 0.76.

Measured on other voices (macOS say and Gemini TTS, offline and on a real call):
real "hey dan" scores 0.82-0.98 on 20 of 22 clips, but "hey" with another one
syllable name scores as high ("Hey Ben" 0.97, "Hey Jan" 0.98, "Hey Stan" 0.96,
"Hey Danny" 0.95). No threshold separates them, so a nearby "hey <name>" can
open a turn. Retrain with more voices for the near misses before relying on it.

scratch_that.onnx listens for "scratch that" (the discard command), trained the
same way but with livekit-wakeword's augmentation patched so that negatives sit
at the end of the 2 s window as the positives do (upstream centers them, and a
model then learns where speech ends instead of which words were said). Its known
threshold in the worker is 0.35. Its own evaluation is pessimistic (recall 0.28
at 0.5), but on other voices (macOS say and Gemini TTS) "scratch that" scores
0.50-0.84 on 9 of 10 voices (a Ukrainian-accented one scores 0.01), and near
misses ("catch that", "match that", "sketch that", "watch that", "send it")
stay at 0.05 or under; "Scratch this plan" reaches 0.51, which the worker's
transcript check keeps as words (it does not end the sentence).
